## .NET Performance Analysis: `StringReadability.cs`

**Context:** Called on every user-facing string in a CMS rendering hundreds of dynamic strings — this is a **hot path**.

---

### Scan Execution Checklist

| Recipe | Hits |
|--------|------|
| `.ToLower()/.ToUpper()` without culture | 2 (L97, L102) |
| Chained `.Replace()` (3+) | 0 |
| `params` in signatures | 1 (L213) |
| `.All/.Any(char.` LINQ on chars | 1 (L93) + 1 (L101) |
| LINQ `.Select/.Where/.Cast/.Aggregate` | 4 (L89, L93, L171, L214) |
| `new Regex(` uncached | 0 |
| `GeneratedRegex` (positive) | 2 (L12-13) |
| `RegexOptions.Compiled` (fallback) | 2 (L22, L24) |
| Unsealed non-abstract classes | 3 (L226, L232, L237) |
| Substring/range allocation | 1 (L106 `result[1..]`) |

---

### Findings

#### 1. LINQ Chain in `FromPascalCase` — Per-Call Enumerator + Delegate Allocations (4 instances) 🟡 Moderate

**Impact:** Every call to `FromPascalCase` allocates: 1 `Cast<Match>` enumerator, 1 `Select` enumerator, 1 closure/delegate, plus per-match `value.All(char.IsUpper)` allocates a `StringRuneEnumerator`. On hundreds of strings per page, this is thousands of allocations.

**Files:** `StringReadability.cs:L86-98`, `StringReadability.cs:L101`

**Fix:** Replace with a `foreach` over `MatchCollection` (which is enumerable without `.Cast<>()` since .NET Core) and use a manual char loop instead of `.All()`:

```csharp
internal static string FromPascalCase(string input)
{
    var matches = PascalCaseWordPartsRegex().Matches(input);
    if (matches.Count == 0) return input;

    var sb = new ValueStringBuilder(stackalloc char[128]);
    for (int i = 0; i < matches.Count; i++)
    {
        if (i > 0) sb.Append(' ');
        var value = matches[i].ValueSpan;
        bool allUpper = true;
        foreach (char c in value)
            if (!char.IsUpper(c)) { allUpper = false; break; }

        if (allUpper && (value.Length > 1 ||
            (matches[i].Index > 0 && input[matches[i].Index - 1] == ' ') ||
            (value.Length == 1 && value[0] == 'I')))
        {
            sb.Append(value);
        }
        else
        {
            foreach (char c in value)
                sb.Append(char.ToLowerInvariant(c));
        }
    }

    var result = sb.AsSpan();
    // Check if all-upper result
    bool needsLower = result.Contains(' ');
    if (needsLower)
    {
        bool allUpperResult = true;
        foreach (char c in result)
            if (c != ' ' && !char.IsUpper(c)) { allUpperResult = false; break; }
        if (allUpperResult)
        {
            Span<char> lower = result.Length <= 128
                ? stackalloc char[result.Length]
                : new char[result.Length];
            result.ToLowerInvariant(lower);
            lower[0] = char.ToUpper(lower[0]);
            return new string(lower);
        }
    }

    if (result.Length > 0)
        sb.GetSpan()[0] = char.ToUpper(result[0]);

    return sb.ToString();
}
```

**Caveat:** `ValueStringBuilder` is an internal runtime type — use `StringBuilder` or bring in a community `ValueStringBuilder` implementation. On .NET 7+ you can also use `Match.ValueSpan` to avoid allocating `.Value`.

---

#### 2. `params IStringTransformer[]` Allocates Array on Every Call 🟡 Moderate

**Impact:** Each call to `.Transform(To.TitleCase)` allocates a single-element `IStringTransformer[]`. Called from `Pascalize()` which is called from `Dehumanize()` per word.

**Files:** `StringReadability.cs:L213`

**Fix:** Add a 1-argument overload to avoid the array allocation:

```csharp
public static string Transform(this string input, IStringTransformer transformer) =>
    transformer.Transform(input);
```

---

#### 3. `Dehumanize` → `Humanize().Pascalize()` Per-Word Cascade 🟡 Moderate

**Impact:** For each word, `Dehumanize` calls `Humanize()` (regex + LINQ + allocations) then `Pascalize()` (which calls `Humanize()` again + `Transform(To.TitleCase)` + `.Replace(" ", "")`). A 5-word string triggers 10+ regex evaluations and ~30 intermediate string allocations.

**Files:** `StringReadability.cs:L171`, `StringReadability.cs:L187`

**Fix:** `Dehumanize` for already-split words only needs title-casing — skip the Humanize round-trip:

```csharp
public static string Dehumanize(this string input)
{
    var words = input.Split(' ', StringSplitOptions.RemoveEmptyEntries);
    if (words.Length == 0) return input;

    var sb = new StringBuilder(input.Length);
    foreach (var word in words)
    {
        sb.Append(char.ToUpper(word[0]));
        sb.Append(word, 1, word.Length - 1);
    }
    return sb.ToString();
}
```

---

#### 4. `.ToLower()` Without Invariant Culture 🟡 Moderate

**Impact:** Allocates a new string per call and uses culture-sensitive casing (Turkish-I bug risk). Called per regex match inside `FromPascalCase`.

**Files:** `StringReadability.cs:L97`, `StringReadability.cs:L102`

**Fix:** Use `ToLowerInvariant()` or write directly into a span with `char.ToLowerInvariant(c)` per char (zero-alloc in the span-based rewrite above).

---

#### 5. Substring Allocation via Range Operator `result[1..]` 🟡 Moderate

**Impact:** Allocates a new string (length-1 chars) on every `FromPascalCase` call just to prepend one uppercase char.

**Files:** `StringReadability.cs:L106`

**Fix:** Use the existing `Concat(char, ReadOnlySpan<char>)` helper already defined in this file:

```csharp
return result.Length > 0
    ? Concat(char.ToUpper(result[0]), result.AsSpan(1))
    : result;
```

---

#### 6. `FreestandingSpacingCharRegex` Called on Every Input ℹ️ Info

**Impact:** Regex evaluation on every input string to detect the rare case of "pure whitespace" input. For a CMS rendering real content strings, this almost never matches.

**Files:** `StringReadability.cs:L55`

**Fix:** Replace with a simple span-based check:

```csharp
if (input.AsSpan().IndexOfAnyExcept(' ', '\t') < 0)
    return input;
```

**Caveat:** The regex also matches Unicode `\p{Zs}` category (non-breaking space, etc.). If that's needed, use `char.GetUnicodeCategory()` in a loop.

---

#### 7. Unsealed Internal Classes (3 instances) ℹ️ Info

**Impact:** Prevents JIT devirtualization of `IStringTransformer.Transform()` calls.

**Files:** `StringReadability.cs:L226` (ToTitleCase), `L232` (ToLowerCase), `L237` (ToUpperCase)

**Fix:** Add `sealed` keyword to all three classes.

---

### Positive Findings

- ✅ `[GeneratedRegex]` used with `#if NET7_0_OR_GREATER` conditional — correct dual-targeting
- ✅ `RegexOptions.Compiled` for down-level — appropriate fallback
- ✅ `Concat(char, ReadOnlySpan<char>)` uses `stackalloc` — good pattern (but not utilized at L106)
- ✅ `IStringTransformer` instances cached as static properties — no per-call allocation of transformers

---

### Summary

| Severity | Count | Top Issue |
|----------|-------|-----------|
| 🔴 Critical | 0 | — |
| 🟡 Moderate | 5 | LINQ chain in `FromPascalCase` (per-call enumerators + delegates × hundreds of strings) |
| ℹ️ Info | 2 | Regex for whitespace check, unsealed classes |

**Estimated per-page savings:** Eliminating findings 1–5 would remove ~15–30 allocations per string × hundreds of strings = **thousands of avoided allocations per page render**. The `Dehumanize` cascade (finding 3) is the worst multiplier since it re-enters the full pipeline per word.

